## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to design a practical, testable fair-use layer for a multi-tenant LLM gateway with billing considerations.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant quotas and burst handling using a token bucket
- Isolation of prompts and responses per tenant
- Simple REST API for quota status and consumption
- Observability and a minimal test plan for bucket behavior

## Code Example
```javascript
class TokenBucket {
  constructor(capacity, refillPerMs) {
    this.capacity = capacity;
    this.tokens = capacity;
    this.refillPerMs = refillPerMs;
    this.lastRefill = Date.now();
  }
  tick() {
    const now = Date.now();
    const elapsed = now - this.lastRefill;
    const refill = Math.floor(elapsed * this.refillPerMs);
    if (refill > 0) {
      this.tokens = Math.min(this.capacity, this.tokens + refill);
      this.lastRefill = now;
    }
  }
  tryConsume(n = 1) {
    this.tick();
    if (this.tokens >= n) {
      this.tokens -= n;
      return true;
    }
    return false;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Real-world needs balance cost, latency, and reliability across tenants. This asks for a concrete policy language, a routing strategy, and test plans under varying traffic. It also probes drift handling and failover.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant QoS policies
- Multi-model routing with cost/latency prediction
- Data locality and failover strategies
- Observability: audit logs, drift tests

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo routing sketch
function route(req, tenant) {
  const p = policy[tenant];
  const candidates = models.filter(m => p.preferredModels.includes(m.name));
  // pick cheapest that meets latency
  return candidates
    .map(m => ({m, est: predict(m, req)}))
    .filter(x => x.est.lat <= p.max_latency)
    .sort((a,b) => a.est.cost - b.est.cost)[0]?.m;
}
```

